artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a federated learning system in which a central server coordinates training over a large population of heterogeneous clients. In each communication round, only a subset of clients can participate, and participation eligibility depends on real-world constraints including: (i) network connection quality and bandwidth, (ii) device type and hardware capability, (iii) geographic location and time zone, (iv) user activity patterns (e.g., charging, idle state), (v) local dataset size, (vi) communication and computation latency, and (vii) the possibility of dropout mid-round. These constraints induce a non-uniform, time-correlated sampling distribution over clients that is generally not under the server's control.

Define a notion of fairness over clients (or over groups defined by protected attributes attached to clients), for example: (a) accuracy parity — the variance or worst-case gap of per-client test accuracy across the population; (b) representation parity — the discrepancy between a client's expected participation frequency and a target uniform or proportional frequency; or (c) group fairness measures such as demographic parity or equalized odds evaluated on the resulting global model.

Address the following:

1. Provide a quantitative characterization of how each of the seven sampling constraints above biases the empirical training distribution seen by the global model, and prove bounds relating the induced sampling bias to the chosen fairness measure of the trained model under standard convex / non-convex federated optimization assumptions.

2. Design federated training procedures that provably mitigate this unfairness, allowing one or more of the following mechanisms:
   (a) modified federated objectives (e.g., minimax / α-fair / agnostic objectives) that reweight client losses;
   (b) biased or importance-weighted client sampling schemes that correct for availability;
   (c) scheduling policies that decide which available clients to admit per round subject to latency / dropout constraints;
   (d) online fairness algorithms that adapt weights or sampling probabilities based on observed participation and per-client performance.

3. For each proposed mechanism, establish convergence guarantees and fairness guarantees (upper bounds on the fairness gap, or impossibility / lower-bound results) as a function of the heterogeneity of constraints, the number of rounds, and the participation pattern.
